[{"id": "f2fc7010-5746-4fc0-9774-c33b3a0c5635", "text": "ADVANCED DATA STRUCTURE  \nWEEK 5 \n5(a) \nAim: To implement binomial heap  \nDescription : The problem requires implementing a binomial heap to efficiently process a \nsequence of priority-queue operations during a hackathon scenario. Starting with an empty heap, \nthe program must handle multiple test cases, where each test case consists of insert operations to \nadd integers into the heap and extract-minimum operations to remove and output the smallest \nelement currently present. The heap must always maintain the binomial heap properties by \norganizing elements as a collection of binomial trees and merging trees of equal degree when \nrequired. For every extract operation, the minimum element among all root nodes is printed, and \nif the heap is empty, the output should be EMPTY. \nAlgorithm: \nAlgorithm: INSERT(x) in Binomial Heap \nInput: An element x \nOutput: Binomial heap after inserting x \n1. Create a new node with key x. \n2. Initialize its degree as 0, and set its parent, child, and sibling to NULL. \n3. Create a temporary binomial heap H₁ containing only this node. \n4. Merge the root list of the current heap H with the root list of H₁ in increasing order of \ndegree. \n5. Traverse the merged root list: \no If two consecutive trees have the same degree, link them. \no Compare their root keys and make the smaller key the parent. \no Increase the degree of the resulting tree by 1. \n6. Continue merging until no two binomial trees have the same degree. \n7. Return the updated binomial heap. \n \n \n \n", "embedding": {"advanced": 0.004032258064516129, "data": 0.004032258064516129, "structure": 0.004032258064516129, "week": 0.004032258064516129, "5": 0.004032258064516129, "5(a)": 0.004032258064516129, "aim:": 0.004032258064516129, "to": 0.020161290322580645, "implement": 0.004032258064516129, "binomial": 0.036290322580645164, "heap": 0.04032258064516129, "description": 0.004032258064516129, ":": 0.0